.SH Step modes
The debugger supports five execution modes, controlled by the client:
.PP
\f[B]run\f[R]: Normal execution.
No single\-stepping; only breakpoints can pause execution.
.PP
\f[B]step\f[R] (step into): Execute one command and pause.
If the command contains nested commands (e.g., a pipeline or compound
command), the debugger pauses at the first inner command.
.PP
\f[B]next\f[R] (step over): Execute one command at the current depth and
pause.
Nested commands at deeper depths execute without pausing.
The debugger records the current command nesting depth and only triggers
when execution returns to the same or shallower depth.
.PP
\f[B]finish\f[R] (step out): Continue execution until the current
nesting level completes.
The debugger pauses when execution returns one level above the depth at
which \f[CR]finish\f[R] was issued.
.PP
\f[B]skip\f[R]: The client acknowledges the breakpoint but allows the
command to execute without further debugger interaction.
Note: due to the hook\-based architecture, the command still executes
even when \(lqskipped\(rq \(en the limitation is that skip does not
suppress execution, it only resumes without pausing.
.SH Pre\-command hook
The debugger registers a pre\-command hook via the
\f[CR]register_pre_command_hook()\f[R] infrastructure.
Before each command executes, the hook:
.IP "1." 3
Checks whether any enabled breakpoint matches the pending command
.IP "2." 3
Checks whether the current step mode requires a pause
.IP "3." 3
If either condition is true, sends a \f[CR]break_hit\f[R] event to the
client on CHAN_DEBUG and enters a blocking read loop waiting for a
resume command
.SH break_hit event
When execution pauses, the server sends a \f[CR]break_hit\f[R] message
containing:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]line\f[R] \(en Source line number of the paused command
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]command\f[R] \(en String representation of the command
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]depth\f[R] \(en Current command nesting depth
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]breakpoint_id\f[R] \(en ID of the triggered breakpoint (if
applicable)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]reason\f[R] \(en Why execution paused
(\f[CR]\(dqbreakpoint\(dq\f[R], \f[CR]\(dqstep\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dqnext\(dq\f[R], \f[CR]\(dqfinish\(dq\f[R])
.SH Client interaction while paused
While execution is paused at a \f[CR]break_hit\f[R], the client can send
the following messages on CHAN_DEBUG:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]set_breakpoint\f[R]: Add a new breakpoint
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]delete_breakpoint\f[R]: Remove a breakpoint by ID
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]enable_breakpoint\f[R] / \f[B]disable_breakpoint\f[R]: Toggle
breakpoint state
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]list_breakpoints\f[R]: Enumerate all breakpoints with their
properties and hit counts
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]inspect_ast\f[R]: Serialize the pending COMMAND structure to JSON
via \f[CR]cmd_serialize()\f[R]; returns the full AST of the command
about to execute
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]step\f[R] / \f[B]next\f[R] / \f[B]finish\f[R] / \f[B]continue\f[R]:
Set the step mode and resume execution
.SH AST inspection
The \f[CR]inspect_ast\f[R] command serializes the pending COMMAND tree
to JSON using \f[CR]cmd_serialize()\f[R] from
\f[CR]cmd_serialize.c\f[R].
This provides a structured view of the parsed command, including:
.IP \(bu 2
Command type (simple, pipeline, connection, group, for, while, if, case,
function_def, etc.)
.IP \(bu 2
Word lists (arguments, patterns)
.IP \(bu 2
Redirections
.IP \(bu 2
Connector types (\f[CR];\f[R], \f[CR]&&\f[R], \f[CR]||\f[R],
\f[CR]|\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
Nested command structures
.PP
The pending command is set via \f[CR]debug_set_pending_cmd()\f[R] before
the pre\-command hook fires.
.SH Conditional breakpoints
The breakpoint structure includes a \f[CR]condition\f[R] field intended
for conditional expressions that must evaluate to true for the
breakpoint to fire.
This field exists in the data structure but condition evaluation is not
yet implemented; all breakpoints with a condition currently fire
unconditionally.
.SH Safety model
Because each client session runs in a forked child process, pausing
execution in the debugger blocks only that session.
The server\(cqs accept loop and all other sessions continue operating
normally.
There is no risk of deadlocking the server by leaving a debugger session
paused.
